>>__ load "simpExtLib";
>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `xtraversedata_for_ss`

``` hol4
simpExtLib.xtraversedata_for_ss :
  simpExtLib.simpset -> TraverseExt.xtraverse_data
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Extracts a configured extension traversal description paired with subgoaler,
unsafe solvers, condition depth and term order. Its native rewriter is
built by `TraverseExt.CONFIG_REDUCER`; supplied extension decision
procedures are unchanged. The data contains equality, traversal rules and the
set's limit.

Pass the pair to `TraverseExt.XTRAVERSE`. For configured reducer data alone,
use `#1 (simpExtLib.xtraversedata_for_ss ss)`. The extension default projection
`simpExtLib.traversedata_for_ss` returns `TraverseExt.traverse_data` with an
explicitly lifted native rewriter and omits strategy settings. Neither is a
projection into ordinary `Traverse` or an import of an ordinary simpset.

Extraction invokes no collectors, recompiles no rules and shares installed
bounded controls without resetting them. `XTRAVERSE pair facts` refreshes
private contexts on each application, even with default settings.
`TraverseExt.TRAVERSE` initializes contexts at construction instead; taking
`#1` neither transports the configuration nor changes that lifetime.

### Failure

Construction or application can fail as for the selected traversal.

### Example

``` hol4
>>_ val ext_bool_ss = simpExtLib.lift_simpset boolSimps.bool_ss;
>>_ val ss = simpExtLib.set_cond_depth 2 ext_bool_ss;
>>_ val pair = simpExtLib.xtraversedata_for_ss ss;
>> val TraverseExt.ConfigReducer =
     TraverseExt.reducer_kind (hd (#rewriters (#1 pair)));
>> TraverseExt.XTRAVERSE pair [] “T ∧ p”;
```

Ordinary simpsets use `simpLib.traversedata_for_ss` and `Traverse`.
Extension callers use the default or configured projection of their
already tracked object; there is no ordinary projection of an extension
simpset.

Start with an extension recipe before installing source-sensitive rules.
There is no transparent import from an ordinary simpset: recompiling known
recipes creates new identities and declared allowances, not the original
remaining quotas. `ssfrags_of` alone also loses history; see
`simpExtLib.mk_simpset` for construction and rebuilding requirements.

### See also

[`simpExtLib.set_cond_depth`](#simpExtLib.set_cond_depth),
[`simpLib.traversedata_for_ss`](#simpLib.traversedata_for_ss),
[`simpExtLib.mk_simpset`](#simpExtLib.mk_simpset).
